  {
    id: 'uc2',
    title: '祖母的菜市场早晨',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 12,
    text: 'At fourteen I decided that selling vegetables was the easiest job in the world, and I asked my grandmother if I could ___1___ her for a morning. She agreed and let me sit behind her table. My first ___2___ walked away before I opened my mouth, and I saw my own ___3___ face in the window behind us. The hardest part was not the work but the ___4___ of waiting for someone to stop, so I gave up trying to sell and started to watch. She was never loud, yet her voice cut through the noise and found the people she wanted to ___5___. She did not simply hand over the tomatoes; she told buyers which ones would taste sweetest. When a boy asked for a free pepper, she gave him one with a smile. “Business,” she said, “is not only about ___6___; it is about whether people feel welcome when they leave.” By ten my throat was dry and my legs shook, but the line of people kept me in my ___7___. At noon a man with a restaurant nearby stopped and said that he needed a supplier he could ___8___. When he asked the price, my first large ___9___ was more than I had imagined, and on the way home the empty cart felt strangely ___10___. I understand now that patience and kindness are not soft qualities but a kind of ___11___ few people learn in a classroom. Her rules are still the ones I ___12___: listen before you speak, and look for the ___13___ behind every small action. Trust grows slowly, like a plant, and can be ruined in a single careless ___14___. That is the ___15___ I took from one hard morning on a plastic chair.',
    questions: [
      { n: 1, opts: ['answer', 'replace', 'help', 'forgive'], a: 2, explain: '上下文题。下文说我坐在桌后学着卖菜，说明我是去“帮忙”，不是替代或原谅她。' },
      { n: 2, opts: ['teacher', 'customer', 'officer', 'reporter'], a: 1, explain: '复现题。全文在菜市场卖菜，走到摊前又离开的人只能是顾客，与后文 buyers 同类。' },
      { n: 3, opts: ['helpless', 'careful', 'familiar', 'cheerful'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。我一句话没说就把人放走了，脸上的神情应是无助的，与后文手脚发麻同向。' },
      { n: 4, opts: ['fun', 'pain', 'noise', 'habit'], a: 1, explain: '逻辑关系题。not…but 构成对比，硬椅子与干渴的喉咙都是折磨，fun 方向相反。' },
      { n: 5, opts: ['miss', 'judge', 'follow', 'reach'], a: 3, explain: '固定搭配题。声音穿过喧闹“够得着”她想吸引的人，reach 与前句 found 呼应。' },
      { n: 6, opts: ['manners', 'profit', 'trouble', 'weather'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。祖母谈的是生意，not only 后接赚钱这一层，与 welcome 构成对比。' },
      { n: 7, opts: ['corner', 'window', 'chair', 'office'], a: 2, explain: '复现题。前文写我坐在桌后，末句又回到 plastic chair，人没走我只能继续坐着。' },
      { n: 8, opts: ['owe', 'punish', 'trust', 'suspect'], a: 2, explain: '上下文题。餐馆老板要找长期供货的人，关键在可靠；suspect 与 punish 与语境相反。' },
      { n: 9, opts: ['bill', 'order', 'menu', 'dish'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。整篮蔬菜被买走，这是我第一次接到大批“订货”；bill 与 menu 属于餐馆端。' },
      { n: 10, opts: ['heavy', 'light', 'cold', 'slow'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。车已卖空却“奇怪地轻”，strangely 提示与前文的沉重形成反差。' },
      { n: 11, opts: ['problem', 'feeling', 'strength', 'secret'], a: 2, explain: '逻辑关系题。not…but 把耐心与善意从“软素质”翻转为一种真正的力量。' },
      { n: 12, opts: ['break', 'ignore', 'keep', 'change'], a: 2, explain: '固定搭配题。keep the rules 遵守规则，冒号后是她留下的两条规矩。' },
      { n: 13, opts: ['cost', 'reason', 'blame', 'danger'], a: 1, explain: '上下文题。先听再说，并寻找小动作背后的“原因”，与前句 listen before you speak 并列。' },
      { n: 14, opts: ['move', 'meal', 'stranger', 'morning'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。a careless move 一步疏忽；本句用植物的比喻说明信任毁于一时。' },
      { n: 15, opts: ['lesson', 'ticket', 'holiday', 'distance'], a: 0, explain: '复现题。全文收束在一个收获上：那一个早晨给我上的“一课”。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'uc3',
    title: '遗忘也是一种能力',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 12,
    text: 'Your brain does not keep everything that reaches it. Every night, while you sleep, it decides which events to store and which to throw away. Scientists once treated this cleaning as a ___1___ of memory, but newer research suggests that forgetting is just as ___2___ as remembering. In one study, researchers trained mice to fear a certain sound. Some animals carried a changed gene that made forgetting impossible; a week later they still ___3___ the sound and could not move freely through the room. Dropping old information lets the brain concentrate on what matters now. If you kept every breakfast of your life, the ___4___ of finding the one from yesterday would be terrible. Teachers have long known something close to this. A student who reads a text again every day often remembers ___5___ than one who waits three days and returns to it, because the effort of ___6___ a half-faded memory makes it stronger. Forgetting also protects our feelings, since the embarrassment of a small mistake fades faster than the skill gained that week. Without this slow washing, failures would stay bright forever and make any ___7___ painful. Some researchers therefore describe memory less as a ___8___ that records every event than as a living muscle that grows when it is used. What happens to it is partly under your control. Sleep is the first ___9___, because the cleaning runs only in the quiet hours, and exercise helps by carrying blood and fuel to the working tissue. Mood matters as well: people who feel safe ___10___ more of what they see. So if one week of study seems to disappear, do not panic. Test yourself, sleep longer, and the missing part is easier to ___11___ than you expect. Fewer ___12___ readings and more active self-testing will serve you better. The aim is not a perfect record but a mind that holds what is useful and lets the rest ___13___ away. An empty afternoon then stops feeling like waste and starts to feel like part of the ___14___, because a brain that deletes well is a busy one, deciding where to spend its limited ___15___ on the things you will need next year.',
    questions: [
      { n: 1, opts: ['weakness', 'treasure', 'decision', 'message'], a: 0, explain: '逻辑关系题。but newer research 把旧看法与今天的评价对照，过去人们把遗忘当作记忆的“缺陷”。' },
      { n: 2, opts: ['strange', 'useful', 'quick', 'hidden'], a: 1, explain: '复现题。as…as 把遗忘与记住并列，后文 also protects 继续说明遗忘同样有用。' },
      { n: 3, opts: ['enjoyed', 'ignored', 'feared', 'produced'], a: 2, explain: '上下文题。这些老鼠无法遗忘，一周后仍然害怕那个声音，前文 trained mice to fear 直接复现。' },
      { n: 4, opts: ['chance', 'prize', 'mystery', 'task'], a: 3, explain: '词义辨析题。记住所有早餐，反而要在海量记录里找昨天那一顿，成了一项糟糕的“任务”。' },
      { n: 5, opts: ['less', 'more', 'better', 'faster'], a: 0, explain: '逻辑关系题。than 前后对比，后句给出原因：费力提取才算真练习，天天重读记得更少。' },
      { n: 6, opts: ['printing', 'rebuilding', 'copying', 'avoiding'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。把半褪的记忆重新“搭建”起来才让它更牢，print 与 copy 都是不费力的重复。' },
      { n: 7, opts: ['risk-taking', 'homework', 'reading', 'sleeping'], a: 0, explain: '复现题。失败若永远鲜明，人就不敢再尝试，前句 a small mistake 与本句 painful 指向冒险。' },
      { n: 8, opts: ['bridge', 'camera', 'library', 'engine'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。that records every event 是照相机式的记录，与后句 living muscle 形成对照。' },
      { n: 9, opts: ['proof', 'example', 'requirement', 'result'], a: 2, explain: '上下文题。本段讲你能控制的条件，睡眠排在第一位，是必要的“条件”而非结果。' },
      { n: 10, opts: ['forget', 'repeat', 'regret', 'remember'], a: 3, explain: '词义辨析题。情绪安全时人记得更多，本段主语 people who feel safe 决定方向。' },
      { n: 11, opts: ['recover', 'explain', 'prove', 'imagine'], a: 0, explain: '复现题。前文说一周的学习似乎消失了，消失的部分其实更容易重新找回。' },
      { n: 12, opts: ['careful', 'repeated', 'short', 'boring'], a: 1, explain: '复现题。全文反对天天重读，fewer 后接“重复的”阅读，与前文 every day 呼应。' },
      { n: 13, opts: ['pass', 'fly', 'wash', 'give'], a: 0, explain: '固定搭配题。holds what is useful 与 lets the rest pass away 对照，pass away 指自然消逝。' },
      { n: 14, opts: ['exam', 'plan', 'process', 'topic'], a: 2, explain: '复现题。文章开头把遗忘称为 this cleaning，空白午后其实是这一“过程”的一部分。' },
      { n: 15, opts: ['money', 'time', 'space', 'energy'], a: 3, explain: '固定搭配题。limited energy 指大脑把有限的“精力”投向明年真正用得上的事。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'uc4',
    title: '学校该不该只奖励第一名',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 12,
    text: 'Many schools put one student at the top of every list and invite the rest to watch. For years this seemed ___1___, because ranking is cheap, fast and easy to explain to parents. Yet more teachers now doubt that a prize for the strongest individual tells us how much a school has taught. The first doubt is about fairness. A rank compares people who began from different places. A pupil who has studied English since the age of three stands far ahead of one who arrived last year and can barely read the question. When we praise the first and say nothing to the second, we are not measuring teaching; we are measuring ___2___. The second doubt is about the lesson inside the ceremony. If the highest reward always falls on someone who beat his classmates, the message is that other people are ___3___ to be used rather than partners to be trusted. Cooperation then disappears from group work, because helping a weaker classmate costs time and brings no ___4___ to the helper. A third doubt concerns the world outside the gate. Modern work rarely rewards a single voice. A bridge is designed by engineers who argue, check each other and write one shared report, and a hospital ward runs on nurses who pass information ___5___ to doctors. There the question is not who stands first, but whether a team can turn a mistake into a plan before the mistake turns into a ___6___. The honest answer is not to throw away testing. Exams show a young person where his work stands and reveal what has not been ___7___. What should change is the number of prizes and the size of the ___8___ behind each one. A school can give more attention to progress, to the essay rewritten after ___9___, and to the group that solved a problem no member could solve alone. Measuring growth is harder than measuring rank, since growth must be watched over months by people who know the work. It asks teachers to write comments rather than point at a ___10___. Many schools keep the old system because it is cheap and looks ___11___. Still, the choice is ours. A prize is a public statement about what a community values. Every year we place one name in front of a hundred others, we teach a lesson that no textbook will repair. A better school would hand out fewer medals and make room for a quiet kind of ___12___: the willingness to let a classmate finish a sentence before you answer for him. Such a school produces no famous list, but it produces adults who can be ___13___ in a room full of strangers. That is what an education should ___14___, and it is the ___15___ a school makes about the people it wants to become.',
    questions: [
      { n: 1, opts: ['reasonable', 'strange', 'secret', 'dangerous'], a: 0, explain: '逻辑关系题。because 给出理由：排名便宜、快捷、好向家长解释，所以多年人们觉得它合理。' },
      { n: 2, opts: ['effort', 'money', 'courage', 'luck'], a: 3, explain: '上下文题。前句比较的是起点完全不同的学生，我们夸的其实是运气，而不是教学。' },
      { n: 3, opts: ['tools', 'friends', 'models', 'judges'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。rather than partners to be trusted 给出反义对照，被“使用”的只能是工具。' },
      { n: 4, opts: ['answer', 'punishment', 'chance', 'praise'], a: 3, explain: '复现题。本段讲的是奖励，帮助同学要花时间又不会带来荣誉，praise 与 reward 同义复现。' },
      { n: 5, opts: ['politely', 'directly', 'secretly', 'lazily'], a: 1, explain: '上下文题。病房靠护士把信息“直接”交给医生，说明团队需要清楚的交接。' },
      { n: 6, opts: ['plan', 'disaster', 'success', 'habit'], a: 1, explain: '逻辑关系题。turn a mistake into a plan 与 mistake turns into a… 对照，错误没人纠正就成事故。' },
      { n: 7, opts: ['finished', 'repeated', 'understood', 'forgotten'], a: 2, explain: '词义辨析题。考试的作用是显示哪些内容还没被“理解”，与本段 measuring teaching 呼应。' },
      { n: 8, opts: ['rule', 'prize', 'message', 'test'], a: 2, explain: '复现题。上文说奖励会传递信息，该变的是奖品数量和每个奖品背后的 message。' },
      { n: 9, opts: ['competition', 'silence', 'practice', 'advice'], a: 3, explain: '复现题。下文 write comments rather than point at a chart，按建议重写才证明进步。' },
      { n: 10, opts: ['list', 'chart', 'window', 'partner'], a: 1, explain: '固定搭配题。point at a chart 指着图表，与 write comments 对照，rather than 决定取舍。' },
      { n: 11, opts: ['hopeful', 'serious', 'costly', 'peaceful'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。旧系统省成本又“看起来严肃”，这正是它难以被替换的原因。' },
      { n: 12, opts: ['patience', 'bravery', 'honesty', 'curiosity'], a: 0, explain: '上下文题。冒号后解释：让同学把句子说完、不替他回答，这种品质是耐心。' },
      { n: 13, opts: ['hidden', 'heard', 'chosen', 'blamed'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。be heard in a room full of strangers 与全文“能说清自己的想法”相呼应。' },
      { n: 14, opts: ['cost', 'refuse', 'copy', 'produce'], a: 3, explain: '复现题。前句 produces no famous list 与本句并列，说明教育应当“产出”什么样的人。' },
      { n: 15, opts: ['report', 'promise', 'speech', 'letter'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。与前句 a public statement 同义，奖章是学校对自己想成为怎样的社群所作的承诺。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'uc5',
    title: '一场辩论赛教会我的事',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 12,
    text: 'The day I stood up to speak in a school debate, I believed that winning was mostly a matter of talking well. I had forty notes and had practised my opening line until it sounded like a ___1___. My partner, Chen Yu, had filled eleven pages with questions our opponents might raise. I was the faster speaker, and I asked her to leave half of the work to me. The first round went as planned. What I did not notice was that the other team had finished a sentence before mine ended, and the hall ___2___ at my timing. By the third round our opponents had stopped answering my arguments and began answering the questions Chen Yu had written the week before. Their speaker was slow and careful, and every point she made carried a ___3___ from our own school life. I felt the ground move under me. In the final round I looked at my notes and found them useless, because I had written speeches rather than ___4___. We came second. Chen Yu said nothing unkind on the bus home, and that ___5___ was worse than any comment. For a week I kept asking one question: what had she done that I had not? The answer came slowly. She had spent two weeks listening — to our teachers, to younger students, to the guard who counted bicycles at the gate — and she had written down the exact words people used when they explained why they felt ___6___ about a rule. She treated an argument as a house that needs a floor, and a floor needs the voices of the people who stand on it. My own notes were only opinions about my own ___7___. In the next term I asked to help with research instead of speaking, and I learned to build my ___8___ first and my voice afterwards. There is a further lesson. When you listen to someone carefully, that person begins to listen back. A shopkeeper near our school told us how the price of milk had changed and why small shops close one by one, and his numbers were more ___9___ than any chart in our textbook. We quoted no famous writer and no report; we quoted a man who had counted boxes for thirty years, and he came to watch us. We came second again, and I did not mind. What I had wanted was never a medal but the right to say something I could ___10___, and that took longer than any opening line. Speaking is the part of thinking that other people can see. If the part below the surface is ___11___, the visible part will shake the moment a real question arrives. My partner never argued with me about that. She simply kept eleven pages beside my forty notes and let the ___12___ speak. The habit she gave me is smaller than a skill and larger than a ___13___: before I try to change a ___14___, I ask the people inside it what they ___15___.',
    questions: [
      { n: 1, opts: ['question', 'poem', 'mistake', 'number'], a: 1, explain: '复现题。练到像“诗”一样顺口，与开头 line 押韵呼应，说明我只重形式。' },
      { n: 2, opts: ['wept', 'shouted', 'laughed', 'whispered'], a: 2, explain: '上下文题。对手先说完一句，全场哄笑我的时机，与后句 felt the ground move 呼应。' },
      { n: 3, opts: ['warning', 'promise', 'joke', 'secret'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。对手每一点都像对我们自己生活的提醒，carry a warning from 表示“警告意味”。' },
      { n: 4, opts: ['speeches', 'questions', 'reasons', 'examples'], a: 1, explain: '逻辑关系题。rather than 对照：我写的是演讲稿，不是对手真会提出的问题，所以临场无用。' },
      { n: 5, opts: ['silence', 'letter', 'lesson', 'report'], a: 0, explain: '上下文题。她在回程车上什么也没说，那份“沉默”比任何评价都难受，前句 nothing unkind 决定答案。' },
      { n: 6, opts: ['proud', 'angry', 'curious', 'nervous'], a: 1, explain: '复现题。人们解释为何对一条校规不满时用的原话，正是她笔记的来源。' },
      { n: 7, opts: ['kindness', 'luck', 'cleverness', 'courage'], a: 2, explain: '词义辨析题。与首段“我认为获胜只靠会说”相扣，我的笔记只是关于自己聪明的看法。' },
      { n: 8, opts: ['voice', 'ears', 'hands', 'eyes'], a: 1, explain: '复现题。build my ears first and my voice afterwards 是本段主旨：先听，后说。' },
      { n: 9, opts: ['frightening', 'simple', 'encouraging', 'boring'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。牛奶涨价、小店一家家关门，这些数字比课本图表更令人心惊。' },
      { n: 10, opts: ['guess', 'forget', 'defend', 'repeat'], a: 2, explain: '固定搭配题。say something I could defend 说出能为之辩护的话，与下文论证的“地板”意象一致。' },
      { n: 11, opts: ['shallow', 'missing', 'clear', 'foreign'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。水面下的思考太浅，露出的部分一遇真问题就发抖。' },
      { n: 12, opts: ['distance', 'change', 'result', 'difference'], a: 3, explain: '固定搭配题。let the difference speak 让差距自己说话，指她那十一页与我那四十页之别。' },
      { n: 13, opts: ['problem', 'prize', 'money', 'gift'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。smaller than a skill and larger than a prize 与前文“我不再在意那块奖牌”同向。' },
      { n: 14, opts: ['rule', 'club', 'memory', 'habit'], a: 0, explain: '复现题。全文辩论围绕校规展开，change a rule 与后句 the people inside it 相扣。' },
      { n: 15, opts: ['fear', 'owe', 'hope', 'see'], a: 3, explain: '上下文题。改变规则之前先问身处其中的人“看到”什么，与前句 ask 构成宾语。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'uc6',
    title: '一本被弄丢的书',
    exam: '中考',
    mins: 10,
    text: 'Last term I lent my best friend a book. It was a birthday present from my grandfather, and I had read it four times. On Friday I asked if she could bring it on Monday, because we had a reading class. She said yes and ___1___ her bag. On Monday she came to school without her ___2___. At break she walked up to me. “I cannot find the book. I looked in every corner of my room.” I felt my face go ___3___ and said nothing for a long time. I had lent it to her because I ___4___ her, but now I was not sure. For two days we did not speak. Then a boy from Class 3 came to me with the book in his hand. “I took it from the desk behind yours last week. I thought it was mine.” He looked at the floor and did not smile. I stood there holding the book, thinking of the two ___5___ days behind us. She had looked for it in her room, and she had come to me on Monday to tell me the truth. She could have said nothing and let me lose the ___6___ in class, but she did not. When I told her about the boy, she did not laugh or ___7___ me. She only said, “I am glad it is back.” That evening I wrote two lines on a card and put it on her desk: thank you for being ___8___. Since then I have learned to ask two questions before I am angry. Is the story clear? Is there another reason for it? A good ___9___ is not a guess about what a friend is thinking; it is a habit of speaking first. If I had asked one more question on Monday, I would not have lost two days of feeling ___10___. My grandfather would not have cared about the book; he would have cared about ___11___ we treated each other. The book now stays on my shelf, and sometimes I borrow her ___12___ instead. Last Friday she asked if I would read a new story with her, and I said yes without ___13___. I keep the card she left in my bag. I do not use it for ___14___. I use it as a reminder that friendship grows fastest in those who are willing to spend time ___15___.',
    questions: [
      { n: 1, opts: ['cleaned', 'closed', 'lost', 'forgot'], a: 1, explain: '上下文题。她答应了并合上书包，表示书已收进包里，为后文忘带埋下伏笔。' },
      { n: 2, opts: ['homework', 'umbrella', 'schoolbag', 'gloves'], a: 2, explain: '复现题。前一句刚提到书包，周一她来校却没带书包，书自然不在身边。' },
      { n: 3, opts: ['warm', 'pale', 'wide', 'busy'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。心爱的书丢了，脸色发白又不说话，pale 符合难过与生气的心情。' },
      { n: 4, opts: ['missed', 'trusted', 'thanked', 'followed'], a: 1, explain: '逻辑关系题。because 说明借书的原因是信任，but now I was not sure 构成转折。' },
      { n: 5, opts: ['sunny', 'short', 'quiet', 'noisy'], a: 2, explain: '上下文题。前句说两天我们谁也不说话，用 quiet 形容那段冷清的时光。' },
      { n: 6, opts: ['name', 'ticket', 'chance', 'seat'], a: 2, explain: '词义辨析题。她只要不说，我就会在阅读课上失去那次发言机会，lose the chance 与全文线索一致。' },
      { n: 7, opts: ['help', 'shout at', 'blame', 'wait for'], a: 2, explain: '词义辨析题。or 连接两种她都没有做的反应：既没笑我也没责怪我。' },
      { n: 8, opts: ['brave', 'polite', 'clever', 'honest'], a: 3, explain: '复现题。她周一主动来告知真相，卡片感谢的正是这份诚实。' },
      { n: 9, opts: ['question', 'friend', 'story', 'answer'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。分号后说它其实是一种先开口的习惯，主语只能是前文强调的“好问题”。' },
      { n: 10, opts: ['excited', 'sorry', 'tired', 'afraid'], a: 1, explain: '上下文题。冷战那两天我一直在自责，feeling sorry 指这段心情。' },
      { n: 11, opts: ['when', 'how', 'why', 'where'], a: 1, explain: '词义辨析题。祖父在意的不是书，而是我们彼此“怎样”相处，cared about 后接方式。' },
      { n: 12, opts: ['dictionary', 'pen', 'magazine', 'bag'], a: 3, explain: '复现题。风波之后我有时反而向她借东西，与开头的 her bag 呼应。' },
      { n: 13, opts: ['waiting', 'looking', 'asking', 'thinking'], a: 3, explain: '固定搭配题。said yes without thinking 不假思索地答应，说明信任已经恢复。' },
      { n: 14, opts: ['writing', 'reading', 'drawing', 'singing'], a: 0, explain: '词义辨析题。卡片不是拿来写字的，do not use it for writing 与后句 as a reminder 对照。' },
      { n: 15, opts: ['running', 'sharing', 'talking', 'listening'], a: 3, explain: '复现题。全文结论是愿意花时间倾听的人友情长得最快，呼应 ask 与 speaking first。' },
